  "worked_solution": "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ and $w$ are $4$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{4}={w}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{4} & ={z}^{4}{w}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have seen above that $(zw)^{4}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is a $4$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
